           <h3>Safe delivery and previews</h3><p>The administrator-only <strong>Operations</strong> workspace can deploy a Git branch into a new release, validate and start it, switch traffic atomically, and retain the previous release for rollback. Signed HMAC webhooks can trigger the same serialized workflow. Preview hostnames are temporary validation environments and expire automatically.</p>
           <h3>Isolation and Anubis</h3><p>Node.js sites may run in a constrained Docker container with a read-only application mount, separate writable data, resource limits, dropped capabilities, and optional outbound-network blocking. This requires the optional Docker-socket Compose overlay and grants SHAM host-level container control, so use a dedicated host or VM. Anubis can run as a per-site anti-bot sidecar with presets, custom policy, health reporting, and bypass behavior. It can disrupt APIs, accessibility tooling, archivers, and legitimate bots; configure exclusions first. It is not a replacement for upstream DDoS protection.</p>
           <h3>Secrets, jobs, and attached services</h3><p>Per-site environment values and reusable database connection profiles are encrypted at rest; secret values are never returned to the browser. Scheduled jobs use bounded five-field cron expressions, prevent overlapping runs, enforce a timeout, and capture output. Restart or redeploy a site after changing runtime configuration.</p>
-          <h3>Backups and updates</h3><p>Operations supports local, restic, S3-compatible, and SFTP backups. Restic provides repository encryption; other destinations rely on their storage encryption unless wrapped by restic. Safe SHAM updates are extracted and signature-checked off the main thread, require explicit acknowledgement when unsigned, create a managed-file backup, and roll back after failed startup. Dependency-changing updates must be installed through a normal reviewed release.</p><h3>Dependency security</h3><p>The container build fails when <code>npm audit</code> reports a high- or critical-severity production dependency. ZIP parsing uses adm-zip 0.6.0, Multer is pinned to 2.2.0, and nested multipart field parsing is disabled on every upload endpoint. Authentication uses jsonwebtoken 9.0.3 so its JWS dependency resolves to the patched 4.0.1 line. Keep a reviewed lockfile in published builds and rerun the audit whenever dependencies change.</p><h3>License and warranty</h3><p>SHAM is licensed under <strong>GPL-3.0-or-later</strong> and is provided without warranty. The complete license is available from the information icon in the sidebar or at <a href="/LICENSE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/LICENSE</a>.</p>
+          <h3>Backups and updates</h3><p>Operations supports local, restic, S3-compatible, and SFTP backups. Restic provides repository encryption; other destinations rely on their storage encryption unless wrapped by restic. Safe SHAM updates are extracted and signature-checked off the main thread, require explicit acknowledgement when unsigned, create a managed-file backup, and roll back after failed startup. Dependency-changing updates must be installed through a normal reviewed release.</p><h3>Dependency security</h3><p>The container build fails when <code>npm audit</code> reports a high- or critical-severity production dependency. ZIP parsing uses adm-zip 0.6.0, Multer is pinned to 2.2.0, and nested multipart field parsing is disabled on every upload endpoint. Authentication uses jsonwebtoken 9.0.3 so its JWS dependency resolves to the patched 4.0.1 line. Keep a reviewed lockfile in published builds and rerun the audit whenever dependencies change.</p><h3>License and warranty</h3><p>SHAM is licensed under <strong>AGPL-3.0-or-later</strong> and is provided without warranty. The complete license is available from the information icon in the sidebar or at <a href="/LICENSE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/LICENSE</a>.</p>
           <h3>Operational routing and observability</h3><p>Sites can define maintenance pages, redirects, custom error responses, cache rules, security headers, and WebSocket-aware edge routing. Runtime logs support search and saved filters; audit logs are exportable. Optional Prometheus, OpenTelemetry, alert destinations, public status, and English, Dutch, or German interface settings are available under Operations.</p>
           <h3>Public endpoints and outbound data</h3><p>The unauthenticated health endpoint returns only a boolean readiness result. Public status is opt-in and publishes service names with coarse health states, never domains, ports, logs, or configuration. Prometheus requires a bearer token and fails closed when no token is saved. Signed deployment webhooks use uniform authentication errors and do not return internal deployment details. Alert, backup, Cloudflare, and OpenTelemetry destinations are administrator-configured trust boundaries; HTTP redirects are rejected before credentials or telemetry are sent.</p>
           <h3>Database and local storage</h3><p>SHAM uses parameterized <code>better-sqlite3</code> statements directly rather than an ORM. SQLite runs with WAL, foreign keys, a busy timeout, bounded retention, and indexes for visitor lookups. Database, upload, snapshot, update, backup, key, and temporary credential files are owner-only on POSIX systems. Backup archives contain the database and encrypted secrets, so protect remote destinations and the encryption keyring together.</p>
